module bindings.ruby.component_base_template_render_without_escaping;

// A view component's markup reaches the page through one class: the framework
// base whose render entry dispatches the compiled template and returns the
// result. Component authors write the markup -- `call` methods and template
// files that interpolate request data -- and the base decides whether that
// output is escaped on the way out, so the base is the unit that either
// applies an HTML escape or does not.
//
// The escape ships as a helper on the same class rather than a call inside the
// render entry itself, which is why the render entry's own body cannot carry
// the discriminator: every remediated spelling still dispatches the template
// renderer from the entry (the current base does exactly that, with the escape
// two helpers away), so a function-scoped binding keyed on the entry's call
// set reports remediated code. The class is the finest scope that holds both
// halves -- the template dispatch it is named for and the escape it owes -- and
// `scope == "class"` reads exactly that.
//
// The escape conjunct is the vocabulary the Ruby `core.HtmlEscape` bindings
// already treat as an HTML escape: `html_escape` (ERB::Util's, ActionView's
// bare spelling and any receiver-qualified form), `escape_html` (Rack::Utils')
// and `escapeHTML` (CGI's). A class carrying any of them has the machinery;
// one carrying none returns whatever the component authored.
//
// Costs and residuals, stated rather than hidden.
//
//   * The conjunct asks whether the class escapes anywhere, not whether it
//     escapes the render pipeline, so an escape applied to some other method
//     of the same class also suppresses. False-negative direction.
//   * `h` -- ERB::Util's short alias -- is too short a needle to key a
//     negation on, and `sanitize` is a tag-stripper rather than an escaper
//     with a name that collides with unrelated methods, so a class escaping
//     through either of those alone stays reported. False-positive direction,
//     and the review flag asks the reader to check the pipeline.
//   * `html_safe` marks a string trusted without escaping it, so it is
//     deliberately not in the conjunct: the remediated base uses it for the
//     default postamble and for annotation markers while the escape itself is
//     `html_escape`, and a class that only marked strings trusted would still
//     deserve the review.
//   * The finding lands on the class declaration rather than the render
//     method, because the render entry is not distinguishable at class scope.

binding componentBaseTemplateRenderWithoutEscaping {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "class"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and node.context.classBase contains "ActionView.Base"
    and node.context.callPath contains "render_template_for"
    and not (containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["html_escape", "escape_html", "escapeHTML"]))
  emit issue code.UnescapedHtmlPresenter at node
}
